  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA front remains stalled over southern Ohio and northern West \nVirginia, with shortwaves and a surface low helping bolster \nshower and thunderstorm activity at times today. Precipitation \nchances linger through Sunday, though coverage should diminish\novernight tonight.\n\nPrecipitable water values hovering around 1.5 to 2 inches will\ncontinue to support rounds of moderate to heavy downpours and \nmaintain flash flooding concerns through this evening. Given \nthe continued uncertainty regarding exact placement of heaviest \nrainfall, a Flood Watch remains in effect for the bulk of the \narea through the evening.\n\nAs for severe potential, a few strong to severe thunderstorms\nare not out of the question today; however, the risk may be\nlessened by cloud cover and earlier activity.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHeat and humidity are expected to increase as an upper ridge\nbuilds in from the west early next week and then becomes \ncentered over the Ohio Valley and central Appalachians from mid\nto late week.\n\nMuch of the area is likely to experience high temperatures in \nthe 90s through the work week and into the start of the holiday \nweekend. In the lowlands, there is 60-90 percent probability \nof at least 90 degree high temperatures from Tuesday through the\nend of the week, and 40-90 percent probability of at least 95 \ndegree high temperatures Wednesday through Friday. Meanwhile, \nheat indices may encroach upon triple digits in the lower \nelevations.",
